  "worked_solution": "In order to find the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta ,$ we calculate the derivative of the log-likelihood function, set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $\\theta .$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative of $l(\\theta ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}l}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\theta }+\\frac{1}{1-\\theta }\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\theta }+\\frac{1}{\\theta -1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we set the derivative equal to $0$ and solve for $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{\\theta }+\\frac{1}{\\theta -1} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{5(\\theta -1)+\\theta }{\\theta (\\theta -1)} & =0 \\\\ 5(\\theta -1)+\\theta  & =0 \\\\ 6\\theta -5 & =0 \\\\ 6\\theta  & =5 \\\\ \\theta  & =\\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for this sample, the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta $ is $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}=\\frac{5}{6}.$",
